    "scope": "Four hosts run private pools, alternating baseline/fixed order; 2 KiB and 8 MiB repeated-byte payloads, standalone/packed files and canonical/file/directory/writer-directory paths. Separate old-writer/new-reader migration and four-client publication/retain/GC/read handoffs. History fixtures contain offline completed receipts, with one real owned 2 KiB file, and use identical log bytes for both binaries. Fresh catalog objects are not cold filesystem caches. Timings include catalog lock/refresh/sync and namespace resolution; no controlled background load, real crash, bandwidth SLA or 100 GB/TB throughput claim. Per sample group n=4 baseline and n=20 fixed; history group n=20 for both; nearest-rank p99 is the maximum."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "Only the cache lookup/remember Rust bodies are proved here, under ENV-STRING-KEY; filesystem resolver, string/path conversion, call sites, replay adapter and whole-system refinement remain unproved.",
    "Namespace model assumes stable alias identities, serialized durable catalog actions and explicit owner release. It does not compose arbitrary graphs, byte recovery or real I/O failures.",
    "Resolved-path identity is not inode identity. Bind-mount aliases are not unified; hardlink names remain distinct and extra unregistered links are not reclaimed.",
    "Alias removal/retargeting and external namespace replacement during retained catalog history are unsupported; cached bindings are not re-resolved to detect these changes.",
    "Identity cache and permanent tombstone/history memory grow with distinct paths/prefixes; fresh-object history replay has measurable extra lookup cost.",
    "Old GC binaries remain unsafe for alias ownership. Migration samples cover old writer/new GC with staging protection during handoff, not arbitrary mixed old collectors or recovery of already lost data.",
    "No cache eviction or controlled background load. Per-group n=4 baseline and n=20 fixed; nearest-rank p99 equals max. Unsafe alias GC timings are not comparable work.",
    "This cycle did not rerun 100 GB publication or physical TB I/O. Previous 100 GB reports use different exact binaries.",
    "Finite tests/model checks and selected function proofs do not establish absence of all bugs."
  ],
